---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Showcase from "../../../components/Showcase.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../../components/StylingHooks.astro";
import "@skryensya/core/components/time-field.css";
import { TimeFieldDemo } from "../../../components/react-demos/time-field";

const install = `@import "@skryensya/core/components/button.css";
@import "@skryensya/core/components/time-field.css";`;

const nativeHtml = `<input type="time" name="start" id="start" />`;

const customHtml = `<div class="sk-time-field" data-sk-time-field data-locale="en-US" data-value="09:30">
  <span class="sk-time-field__label">Departure</span>
</div>`;

const react = `import { TimeField } from "@skryensya/react/time-field";

<TimeField
  defaultValue="09:30"
  label="Departure"
  locale="en-US"
  name="departure"
/>`;
---

<Base
  title="TimeField"
  description="A segmented time field — hour, minute, and AM/PM as independently editable, keyboard-operable parts."
>
  <h1>TimeField</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A field for a local wall-clock time — no date, no time zone. Hour, minute, and (in a 12-hour
    locale) AM/PM are three independently editable segments in one field, instead of the browser's
    own <code>&lt;input type="time"&gt;</code> chrome, which differs enough between Chrome, Firefox
    and Safari that it cannot be styled or relied on to look the same twice. There is no
    <code>@zag-js/time-picker</code> machine, so this component is hand-rolled state, the same way
    Slider and Segmented are — but no popover either: an earlier version put a scroll-wheel picker
    behind a trigger, and it turned out to be neither simpler nor more accessible than building the
    segments directly.
  </p>

  <Showcase html={customHtml} react={react} label="TimeField">
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <TimeFieldDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </Showcase>

  <h2>Contract</h2>
  <p>
    Segment order and the separator between them are read off <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code>'s
    own <code>formatToParts</code>, not assumed: some locales place the day period before the time,
    and the separator is not always <code>":"</code>. The public value is still the canonical
    <code>HH:mm</code> string, the same shape a plain <code>&lt;input type="time"&gt;</code>
    submits — carried on a hidden input, so a form behind TimeField never has to parse a
    locale-dependent string. React holds this as component state (<code>value</code>/<code>defaultValue</code>,
    an <code>onValueChange</code> callback). Vanilla hydrates the authored
    <code>data-sk-time-field</code>: the consumer authors only the root and its label, and the
    component generates the segments from <code>data-locale</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Editing a segment</h2>
  <p>
    Typing a digit fills the focused segment and moves on once no further digit could keep it
    valid — typing <kbd class="sk-kbd">1</kbd> in the hour segment of a 12-hour field waits briefly
    for a possible second digit (<code>10</code>–<code>12</code>), typing <kbd class="sk-kbd">9</kbd>
    advances immediately, since no second digit could follow it and stay ≤ 12. Arrow Up/Down step
    the value and wrap at the ends; <kbd class="sk-kbd">Backspace</kbd> clears the segment;
    <kbd class="sk-kbd">←</kbd>/<kbd class="sk-kbd">→</kbd> move between segments; typing the first
    letter of the locale's AM/PM label on that segment sets it directly.
  </p>

  <h2>If you just need the platform picker</h2>
  <p>
    A plain <code>&lt;input type="time"&gt;</code> still works, needs no CSS or JavaScript, and gets
    the OS's own keyboard and picker for free. Reach for TimeField only once that picker's
    inconsistency across browsers, or its total absence of styling hooks, actually costs you
    something — the same trade DatePicker makes against a plain <code>type="date"</code> input.
  </p>
  <Showcase html={nativeHtml} label="Native time input" />

  <h2>Accessibility</h2>
  <p>
    Each segment is <code>role="spinbutton"</code> inside one <code>role="group"</code> named by
    the field's own label: <code>aria-valuenow</code>/<code>aria-valuetext</code> carry its current
    value (a friendly placeholder like “hh” before anything is set, not a bare empty string), and
    <code>aria-valuemin</code>/<code>aria-valuemax</code> its real range — 1–12 for an hour segment
    in a 12-hour locale, 0–23 in a 24-hour one. Every segment is its own tab stop, the same way a
    native multi-part date input already works, so keyboard use needs nothing beyond Tab and the
    arrow keys documented above.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <pre><code>{install}</code></pre>
  <StylingHooks
    groups={[
      { file: "components/time-field.css", probe: "sk-time-field", title: "TimeField" },
      { file: "components/button.css", probe: "sk-button", title: "Inherited from Button" },
    ]}
  />
</Base>
